{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>These are the reckonings of the Tabernacle.</b> Each of the components of the Tabernacle listed above is one of those reckoned items concerning which it says: וּבְשֵׁמוֹת תִּפְקְדוּ אֶת כְּלֵי מִשְׁמֶרֶת מַשָּׂאָם בְּיַד אִיתָמָר (Numbers 4:32–33). This is because each of them was worthy of being reckoned and called by name as this particular individual, not merely as belonging to this species. How much more does this apply to each of the sacred vessels under the charge of the sons of Kohath. And therefore they were not destroyed, as the Sages said: לֹא יָרְדוּ — 'lest you say their reasoning was lost and their plan annulled, Scripture says: עֲצֵי שִׁטִּים עֹמְדִים — they stand forever and ever' (Numbers 4:32). And likewise nothing of them fell into the enemies' hand, the opposite of what happened to Solomon's Temple and its vessels, as explained in the account of the destruction of the First Temple at the hand of Nebuzaradan, where nothing concerning the Tabernacle of Moses our Master, peace be upon him, is mentioned at all." "<b>The Tabernacle of the Testimony.</b> He recounts the merits of this Tabernacle by virtue of which it was worthy of being eternal and of not falling into the enemies' hand. First, that it was the Tabernacle of the Testimony, in which the tablets of the testimony were kept. Second, <b>which was reckoned at the mouth of Moses</b>. Third, that <b>the service of the Levites was under Itamar</b> — for indeed the charge of all the components of the Tabernacle was under Itamar's hand. Fourth, <b>and Bezalel son of Uri son of Hur, of the tribe of Judah, made it</b> — that the master craftsmen of the Tabernacle's work and its vessels were men of pedigree and the righteous of the generation, and therefore the Shekhinah dwelt in the work of their hands and it did not fall into the enemies' hand. But Solomon's Temple, whose workers came from Tyre — even though the Shekhinah dwelt in it, its parts deteriorated, and it required repairs to the House, and in the end fell entirely into the enemies' hand. But the Second Temple, in which not one of all these conditions was present — the Shekhinah did not dwell in it, and it fell into the enemies' hand. For indeed in the Second Temple there was no Tabernacle of the Testimony, since the tablets of the testimony were not in it; nor was it reckoned except by the decree of Cyrus; nor were the sons of Levi there, as Ezra testified, saying: וָאָבִינָה בָעָם וּבַכֹּהֲנִים, וּמִבְּנֵי לֵוִי לֹא מָצָאתִי שָׁם (Ezra 8:15); and among those engaged in its building were Sidonians and Tyrians, as explained in the book of Ezra."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The Sages' saying here does not in fact begin with לֹא יָרְדוּ; that phrase appears to be a copyist intrusion. The quotation opens with שֶׁמָּא תֹּאמַר and runs through עוֹלְמֵי עוֹלָמִים; it is a paraphrase of a well-known midrash on Numbers 4:32. Translated hyper-literally as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:28:29.917660381Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}